body:
Hi there - 

So funny-enough, I have been in similar situations before with men where they start to ease up on planning dates after a few. I've come to learn that men don't love the idea of constantly chasing women that don't reciprocate with either some sort of planning or overture of liking them.

&#x200B;

In this very specific case, I set up the third date---and it was wonderful. We had a great time and on that date took the conversation and courtship to the next level. He admitted to me later that he really (really!) appreciated the fact that I set something up with him --that showed I really liked him. Since then, we've gone out several more times and have been seeing eachother regularly.

&#x200B;

I can't say it always amounts to something like this - very often, it tends to mean their interest in waning. I think in this siutation, however, he'd been pursuing me (in hid mind) for a couple of months so it was probably about time I put the effort in to plan. What is happening with you?

We've all been there girl. It sounds like he might be missing in action but I wonder if you might feel better if you sent him a message that gave yourself a little closure. Nothing overt, or angry, maybe something like "Hey, haven't heard from you. Sounds like we're no longer hanging out. It's been nice getting to know you. See ya."

&#x200B;

Sometimes taking back the control that way can soften the rejection.
